    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d688">

                <pb facs="0620" n="614" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg614"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.8227/1a: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Walton</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-03-25T14:00:00-05:00">March 25, 1939—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9. In connection with the Department’s telegram no. 8 of March 25, 1 p.m.<anchor
                        n="49" xml:id="d688-f1"/> this Government would be willing to initiate
                    negotiations with the Liberian Government for the conclusion of an agreement on
                    civil aviation. You will recall that the aviation provisions of our recently
                    negotiated Treaty of Commerce and Navigation<anchor n="50" xml:id="d688-f2"/>
                    were omitted at the last moment owing to the desire of the Liberian Government
                    to obtain reciprocal most-favored-nation treatment which the United States was
                    unable to grant. If you think the Liberian Government would now look with favor
                    on the negotiation of an agreement embodying such provisions on the subject of
                    civil aviation as the two Governments may be willing to agree upon, you are
                    authorized to broach the matter to the appropriate authorities. Should there be
                    a favorable response, a draft agreement will be sent you.</p>
                <p>For such discreet confidential use as you may deem advisable, it may be observed
                    that the negotiation of an aviation agreement between Liberia and the United
                    States at this time, following closely upon the visit of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Boise</hi>,<anchor n="51" xml:id="d688-f3"/> might serve
                    as an indication of the continued close interest of this Government in the
                    future of Liberia and correspondingly make an impression on any nation with
                    colonial ambitions in West Africa.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d688-f1" n="49" xml:id="d688-f1-content">
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg561">p. 561</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d688-f2" n="50" xml:id="d688-f2-content">
                <p>Signed at Monrovia, August 8, 1938, Department of State Treaty Series No. 956, or
                    54 Stat. 1739. For correspondence relating to the negotiations, see <ref
                        target="frus1938v02#pg_842"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1938, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, pp. 842</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d688-f3" n="51" xml:id="d688-f3-content">
                <p>For correspondence concerning courtesy visit of the U. S. S. <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Boise</hi> to Liberia, October 29 to November 3, 1938,
                    see <ref target="frus1938v02#pg_817"><hi rendition="#italic">ibid.</hi>, pp.
                        817</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
